    {
      "filename": "Haiti, Dominican Republic, and Turks and Caicos (MODIS 2016-12-23).jpg",
      "title": "File:Haiti, Dominican Republic, and Turks and Caicos (MODIS 2016-12-23).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c7/Haiti%2C_Dominican_Republic%2C_and_Turks_and_Caicos_%28MODIS_2016-12-23%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haiti,_Dominican_Republic,_and_Turks_and_Caicos_(MODIS_2016-12-23).jpg",
      "width": 2044,
      "height": 1816,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "artist": "Jeff Schmaltz, MODIS Land Rapid Response Team, NASA GSFC",
      "description": "On December 9, 2016, the Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (MODIS) aboard NASA’s Terra satellite captured a striking true-color image of Haiti, the Dominican Republic, and the Turks and Caicos Islands.\nThe large, long island in the lower part of the image is Hispaniola. Encompassing an area of about 29,128 square miles (76,192 square km), the second-largest island in the Caribbean after Cuba. The island is shared by Haiti (west) and the Dominican Republic (east).\n\nNorth of Haiti and the Dominican Republic lies a cluster of islands known as the Turks and Caicos Islands. Eight of the islands are relatively large, compared to the few dozen smaller islands in the archipelago. All of the low-lying islands sit atop undersea coral pillars, which give the bright blue color to the oceans surrounding the islands.",
      "dateCreated": "Taken on 9 December 2016",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "turks-and-caicos-islands-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "d7ug",
      "mediumPath": "_m/d/7/u/g/turks-and-caicos-islands-wp/haiti-dominican-republic-and-t-medium.webp",
      "smallPath": "_m/d/7/u/g/turks-and-caicos-islands-wp/haiti-dominican-republic-and-t-small.webp"
    },
    {
      "filename": "Hispaniola and Turks and Caicos (MODIS 2018-06-18).jpg",
      "title": "File:Hispaniola and Turks and Caicos (MODIS 2018-06-18).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e4/Hispaniola_and_Turks_and_Caicos_%28MODIS_2018-06-18%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hispaniola_and_Turks_and_Caicos_(MODIS_2018-06-18).jpg",
      "width": 2100,
      "height": 1955,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "artist": "Jeff Schmaltz, MODIS Land Rapid Response Team, NASA GSFC",
      "description": "In the blue waters of the North Atlantic Ocean, north of the large island of Hispaniola, lies a cluster of jewel-like islands. Known as the Turks and Caicos Islands, the group contains more than 40 islands and cays and draw more than a million tourists each year – even though only five of the larger islands and a few of the smaller islands are inhabited. Each of the low-lying islands sit atop undersea pillars of coral and many are surrounded by spectacular coral reefs.\nOn June 15, 2018, the Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (MODIS) aboard NASA’s Terra satellite acquired a true-color image of part of the Turks and Caicos Islands along with a portion of Hispaniola.\n\nBright peacock blue marks a lagoon-like reef system around islands in the Caicos group including, from west to east, Providenciales, North Caicos, Grand Caicos and East Caicos. Further east, brilliant blue also rings Grand Turk Island and the smaller Salt Cay (in the south). In the west the largest island is Inagua Islands and the smaller one in the north is Little Inagua – both part of the Bahamas. Hispaniola lies in the south. The west of this large island belongs to Haiti and the eastern section belongs to the Dominican Republic.",
      "dateCreated": "Taken on 15 June 2018",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "turks-and-caicos-islands-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "d7ug",
